  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1220 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nWednesday begins with overnight convection from Tuesday persisting\ninto the morning hours and additional rounds of rain will continue\nthrough much of the day, moving from SW to NE across the\nPanhandles. Moist and unstable forecast soundings combined with \nhigh precipitable water values across the CWA suggest that heavy \nrainfall will be the primary hazard throughout the day. That\nalongside the possibility of training thunderstorms will promote a\nflash flood threat. The chances for flash flooding are the \nhighest across the southeast TX Panhandle as those areas are most \nfavored to receive >1.00\" QPF totals. A severe thunderstorm threat\ncannot be completely ruled out as along as embedded thunderstorms\ncan form within the stratiform precipitation system. Forecast \nsoundings suggest that a low equilibrium level and elongated \ninstability profiles will make it difficult for severe \nthunderstorm organization. Still, some hail and gusty winds cannot\nbe ruled out with the strongest of thunderstorms on Wednesday. \nThe timing for this low potential severe threat will exist as long\nas day time heating is present; meaning, strong thunderstorms \ncannot be ruled out even during the morning hours.\n\nAs we transition to Thursday, the upper level system should also\ncontinue its transition eastward. Some models have it moving out\nquicker than others at this time, so precipitation chances for the\nHigh Plains are still somewhat ambiguous. If the low pressure\nsystem hangs back, more of our area could be in play for rain\nagain. If it quickly pushes east into the central Plains,\nprecipitation chances will be much more limited. A cold front \nwill also move through on Thursday and introduce cooler, more \nstable air in its wake.\n\nFriday into the weekend, temperatures should be much cooler across\nthe Panhandle. Highs may not even escape the 50s Friday afternoon.\nWe are also watching the potential for freezing temperatures\nSaturday morning, as current forecast lows get down to the mid 30s\nacross our northern zones. \n\nRangel",
